        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>The regulation of the intracellular level of Ca2+ is very crucial for a number of biological functions such as gene expression, muscle contraction, bone remodeling, glucose metabolism, and apoptosis. Calbindins are Ca2+-binding proteins that have among their known functions in cells the buffering of intracellular Ca2+ and thus, inter alia, potentially modulate Ca2+-dependent biological functions in living organisms. In order to better understand the mechanism of action of calbindins including their role in the (1) active absorption of Ca2+ in the kidney, (2) the regulation of insulin producing cells (beta-cells) and (3) glucagon producing cells (alpha-cells) of the pancreas we have used microgravity as a perturbent and have analysed the in situ levels of calbindins in kidney and intestine and levels of insulin and glucagon in the pancreas. The rats were flown aboard the Space Shuttle (STS-40, STS-58, and STS-90) to expose them to microgravity. The in situ levels of calbindins, insulin and glucagons were then determined by quantitative immunocytochemistry coupled to digital video imaging. The study provides microgravity as an important perturbent for understanding signaling mechanisms in living systems.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Quantitative immunocytochemistry (QICC) experiments were carried out in conjunction with the digital video image analysis in order to measure the in situ expression of calbindins, insulin and glucagons from 10% formalin-fixed tissues of flight rats and their controls. Cellular localization of calbindin-D28k was carried out with a monoclonal antibody directed against chicken intestinal calbindin-D28k (clone CL-300, Sigma), which specifically and strongly cross reacts with calbindin-D28k in higher vertebrates, including rats, and an indirect peroxidase method. Cellular localization of calbindin-D9k was carried out with a rabbit anti-rat intestinal calbindin-D9k. Monoclonal antibodies were used to localize insulin and glucagon. Using the quantitative in situ immunocytochemical analysis, differences in percentages of reactive cells and in intensity of the reaction product in specific cells indicate differences in gene expression for the proteins under some microgravity-associated conditions. <P>In situ levels of calbindins, insulin and glucagon were quantified on the basis of the intensity of the oxidized diaminobenzidine reaction product  present in individual cells, using a MetaMorph image acquisition, processing, and  integrated morphometric analysis system (Universal Imaging Corp., PA). Labeling intensities were measured on digitized images in arbitrary average gray level units based on a 256 tone gray scale, where a 0 value indicates completely black and 255 indicates completely white (transparent). The intensity value included both the labeled cells and their individual brightness values, and was obtained by defining the outline of cell clusters using an inclusive thresholding procedure and determining the average gray values over the thresholded areas. The brightness of the background was determined on the entire image by using the exclusive thresholding protocol in which the objects were excluded and specifically the average gray level of the background was counted. Data is expressed as relative labeling intensity and is defined as the inverse of (average gray level of the area of interest/average gray level of the background) x 100, so that the higher numbers correspond to the higher levels of calbindins and pancreatic hormones. QICC was performed blindly, i.e., without knowing initially the protocol for the groups from which the tissue was derived.<P>The insulin and glucagons content in pancreas was also determined by using standard biochemical Enzyme-Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA) protocol.<P><B>Results:</B><P>A summary of the results for the three experiments are listed below.  For a complete discussion and tables see the final report "Calbindins and Apoptosis After Short-term Spaceflight".<P>TASK 1:  To quantify in situ levels of calbindins in the small intestine, hypothalmic nuclei, kidney, and pancreas.<P>Kidney:  analysis by QUICC revealed a significantly higher f (by one way analysis of variance) level of calbindin-D28k in animals in space for 16 days on STS-90 plus 1-day at Earth gravity (R+1) (Figure 1) compared to asynchronous ground control rats ((48 hour delay from experimental group, vivarium, R+3 (P=0.008); 96 hour delay from experimental group, simulation, R+5 (P<0.001)), flight animals plus 14-days at Earth gravity (R+14) (P<0.001), and asynchronous ground controls: 15 days delay, vivarium (R+15) (P=0.004); 17 days delay, simulation (R+17) (P=0.008).<P>Only the flight animals at 1 day following return from on orbit exhibited statistically significant differences from the other groups, including flight animals after 14 days at Earth gravity.<P>Duodenum:  levels of calbindin-D28k determined by QUICC were rather consistent across groups, flight and controls.  No statistically significant differences were found (P>0.06).<P>Hypothalmus: sections of hypothalami from STS-56 showed little or no reactivity for calbindin-D28k. This was an unexpected result since calbindin-D28k has previously been described as present in neurons of the ventromedial nucleus, supraoptic nucleus and medial mammillary nucleus of the rat. Because there was weak immunoreactivity for positive control peptides (neuropeptide Y and neurophysin), it is believed that the lengthy storage time in fixative contributed to the diminished immunoreactivity for calbindin-D28k and the control neuropeptides in the hypothalamus. The flight controls as a group showed weaker neurophysin II immunoreactivity than flight animals, however, the flight group exhibited considerable variability in the density of reaction product. Due to weak or absent immunoreactivity in the available tissue specimens, no conclusion can be reached regarding the effect of mg on hypothalamic levels of calbindin-D28k.<br><br>Pancreas<br>Our results confirmed that there is little or no expression of calbindin-D28k in Beta-cells of normal pancreatic islets of the rat.  There were no observed differences in calbindin-D28k levels in the pancreatic islets of flight and control animals.<P>TASK 2:  To evaluate apoptosis in the small intestine, hypothalmic nuclei, kidney and pancreas.<P>Duodenum: Apoptosis in the duodenum ranged from five to about 11 apoptotic nuclei per three microvillous profiles over all experimental and control groups.  There was no statistically significant difference by ANOVA in apoptotic rates between any groups (P=0.2).<P>Pancreas: For the pancreas, apoptosis was expressed as the number of FITC-positive (apoptotic) Beta-cell nuclei per pancreatic islet.  The apoptotic rate based upon TUNEL staining and morphological criteria was low for all islets examined (0-3).  There was no statistically significant difference by ANOVA in apoptotic rates between any groups.<P>Hypothalmus: Apoptotic nuclei were only infrequently observed in nerve cells of the hypothalmus in flight and control specimens.  There was no statistically significant difference by ANOVA in apoptotic rates between any groups.<P>Kidney: Distal convoluted tubules exhibited few apoptotic nuclei and there was no statistically significant difference in apoptotic rates between any groups.<P>TASK 3:  To determine if levels of renal calbindin-D28k depleted by microgravity are restored to normal after nine- and 14-days at Earth gravity.<P>The results of this study with ELISA of calbindin-D28k did not confirm the reduction in calbindin-D28k found in our earlier study (Sergeev, et al. 1996).  Calbindin-D28k  levels in kidneys of SLS-1 flight animals after just returning to Earth (R+0), 2.24 ± 0.17 ï¿½g/mg protein, were significantly greater than those of basal controls (L+0), 0.89 ± 0.06 ï¿½g/mg, but not ground controls (GC), 1.97 ± 0.03 ï¿½g/mg.  Levels of calbindin-D28k measured by ELISA were equivalent in flight animals immediately following flight (R+0) and after nine days at Earth gravity (R+ML), 1.94 ± 0.12 ï¿½g/mg protein (P=0.5).  In SLS-2 animals, renal calbindin-D28k in flight animals after just returning to Earth (R+0), 2.22 ± 0.27 ï¿½g/mg protein, was significantly greater that basal controls (L+0), 1.08 ± 0.13, (P<0.001) and ground controls, 0.62 ± 0.04 (P<0.001).  Calbindin-D28k in kidneys of flight animals after 14 days of Earth gravity (R+ML), 2.03 ± 0.31 (P=0.06).  Thus, the results of this study indicate an incrrease in calbindin-D28k levels in microgravity.  Differences in the results of this and our previous study (Sergeev, et al., 1996) suggest that other factors the animals are exposed to during flight regulate in a substantial way calbindin-D28k.  Among these factors may be environmental stresses, such as elevated temperatures (from about 24.5 to 28.5 degrees C in SLS-2) and reduced humidity (slightly more than 30 percent to over 100 percent for SLS-2).  Temperature extremes were even greater for SLS-1 (27-32 degrees C).<br><br>TASK 4:  To determine pancreatic levels of insulin.<br><br>Head of pancreas<br>Insulin content of the head of the pancreas from rats flown on STS-58 (SLS-2) was significantly higher (P>0.001) immediately following recovery (R+0 F) 364.7 ± 52.0 ng/mg (n=6), than all of the appropriate controls: R+0 FC (flight control), 160.1 ± 17.8 ng/mg (n=6), R+0 GC (ground control), 104.4 ± 14.0 ng/mg; BC (basal control), 71.2 ± 4.1 ng/mg (n=6).<br><br>The elevated insulin content of flight animals following recovery was also significantly greater (P<0.001) than the content of flight animals held for mission-length following recovery (F, R+ML), 141.7 ± 28.6 ng/mg (n=6), and their controls ((FC, R+ML, 94.1 ± 19.2 ng/mg (n=6); GC, R+ML, 92.4 ± 17.8 ng/mg (n=5)).  After 14-days at Earth-gravity; the insulin content of flight rats (F, R+ML) was similar to control levels (FC R+0; GC, R+0).  There was no significant difference between flight animals held on the ground for the length of the mission after recovery (F, R+ML) and mission-length and recovery flight controls (FC: R+ML and R+0) and ground controls (GC: R+ML and R+0).  There also was no statistically significant difference between mission-length controls (FC, R+0; GC, R+0) and recovery plus mission-length animals.  No other significant differences were present by ANOVA.<br><br>The concentration of insulin in the head of the pancreas from rats flown on STS-40 (SLS-1) tended to be lower than that of STS-58.  The insulin concentration for flight animals from STS-40 "immediately" following recovery (F, R+0) was 103.9 ± 11.9 ng/mg (n=10), which was significantly greater (P<0.02) that flight controls (C, R+0), 58.2 ± 11.0 ng/mg (n=5).  The insulin content of flight animals (F, R+0) was not significantly different from vivarium controls (CV, R+0), 84.9 ± 9.4 ng/mg (n=5) or basal controls (L+0), 83.4 ± 12.1 ng/mg (n=5).  As with rats from the STS-58 flight, there was no difference between flight animals held for mission-length after recovery (F, R+ML), 97.9 ± 14.2 ng/mg (n=9) and recovery plus mission-length control animals (C, R+ML: 67.4 ± 16.3 ng/mg; CV, R+ML: 131.3 ± 9.3 ng/mg).<br><br>Tail<br>Sample of the tail of the pancreas for insulin extraction were only available for STS-58.  In contrast to the findings with the head of the pancreas, the insulin content of the tail of the pancreas from flight animals following landing (R+0), 19.4 ± 47.7 ng/mg of protein (n=6) did not differ significantly from any of its controls: FC, R+0, 202.1 ± 30.0 ng/mg (n=5) (P=0.81); GC. R+0, 235.0 ± 31.2 ng/mg (n=6) (P<0.05); L+0. 153.7 ± 35.8 ng/mg (n=6) (P<0.05).<br><br>In summary, the concentration of insulin in the head of the pancreas is increased following nine and 14 days of exposure to microgravity.  Additional experiments are necessary to evaluate the functional significance of increased insulin levels in the pancreas following return to earth after exposure to microgravity.
        </div>

